<p>Questions to bring to the text (pp. 45–52)</p>
<p>•&nbsp; What does Hegel mean when he says world-historical individuals are “practical, political men” rather than philosophers?</p>
<p>•&nbsp; How does Hegel explain why their contemporaries often fail to appreciate or recognize them?</p>
<p>•&nbsp; Is there a tension between Hegel’s admiration for world-historical individuals and his claim that history moves toward universal freedom?</p>
